WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:08.320
In March 2021, at the height of the coronavirus&nbsp;
pandemic, four of Europe's most powerful women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.480
sent a letter to Brussels.  
 
It included a dire warning. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:17.600
The pandemic had shown how much the EU&nbsp;
depends on foreign technology, they wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.960
— and this needs to change.   
 
Their language was diplomatic — but the underlying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.920
message was clear: It’s time to rein in Big Tech.
“Tech giants will have to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.960
the way they do business…”
"…an unprecedented amount of pressure…”&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.440
“A Day of Reckoning…” 
 
"Mr. Zuckerberg, I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.400
want a Yes or No answer. Mr. Pichai, Yes or No?"&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.520
Governments around the globe have set their&nbsp;
eyes on the world’s largest tech companies.&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.200
But how did Big Tech come under so much fire?
And how did it get so “Big” in the first place?&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:58.720
We might not always notice it — but technology is&nbsp;
all around us. We use it to communicate, to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.880
information, to organize our lives. And almost all&nbsp;
that technology is made by private companies.  
 &nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:12.000
This has given rise to an industry known as Big&nbsp;
Tech. And within this industry, four companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.440
have become especially powerful:  Alphabet —&nbsp;
which owns Google — Amazon, Facebook and Apple. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:26.240
In fact, they’re so big now they’re known as&nbsp;
the “Big Four,” or by their acronym “GAFA.”&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.640
Those “Big Four” actually run fairly different&nbsp;
businesses. But what they have in common is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.560
that they provide digital services — and they have&nbsp;
become so ingrained in our lives that it’s almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.280
impossible to avoid them.  
 
Take me, for example:&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.320
My phone is made by Apple. And when I&nbsp;
use it to surf the internet, that traffic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:50.000
likely runs through servers owned by Amazon.  
 
Or let’s talk about you: There’s a good chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.320
you’re watching this video on YouTube,&nbsp;
which is owned by Google. And you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:58.160
have come across it on Facebook.  
 
The point is: Big Tech is everywhere.&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:04.720
And this has made the companies very, very rich. 
 
While the coronavirus forced many businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.200
to down shutters and plunged the&nbsp;
world economy into a recession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:16.160
Big Tech is reporting enormous profits. 
 
The stock prizes of all Big Four have skyrocketed:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.680
Some, like Apple’s, doubled in value&nbsp;
since the beginning of the pandemic&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.160
But many would argue that this&nbsp;
success comes with responsibility —  
 &nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.240
and increasingly, people are asking if …
"…those company are living up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.760
to that responsibility?"
“No, and I don't think we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.280
should expect of Big Tech to be benevolent.” 
 
“They have been trying but it’s really hard.“ 
&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.440
“The answer to that depends on&nbsp;
where you are talking from.”&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.720
Let’s untangle this. The core criticism&nbsp;
of Big Tech comes down to four points: 
 &nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.360
It’s about how the companies: 
 
—protect the privacy of their users&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.800
—can be held liable for what’s&nbsp;
happening on their platforms&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.880
—pay their taxes and
—how they treat their competitors  
 &nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:03.920
And when it comes to the first point — privacy&nbsp;
— Big Tech has been under fire for years.&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:09.200
But it wasn’t always that way: The birth of&nbsp;
Big Tech dates back to the Silicon Valley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.400
of the late 1990s and early 2000s, when&nbsp;
most of the companies were founded.  
 &nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:20.800
Their missions were ambitious: The startups&nbsp;
were out to disrupt the status quo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.800
connect people and give them&nbsp;
unprecedented access to information.  
 &nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.080
And when a decade later, protests&nbsp;
spread across the Arab world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.760
many saw that as proof for how the technology&nbsp;
could, indeed, be a force for democracy.&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.320
And it was. But what people&nbsp;
didn’t pay much attention to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.600
was how Big Tech actually makes money:  
 
Most of the companies got big by providing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:51.840
free services — while, in exchange, collecting&nbsp;
data about their users and selling ads. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.840
And another couple of years later, the Cambridge&nbsp;
Analytica scandal helped many understand that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:05.520
very data could also be used to manipulate them.
When reports emerged that a consultancy had mined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.640
information about millions of Facebook users&nbsp;
to influence how they voted in the UK’s Brexit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.240
referendum and the 2016 US election, that&nbsp;
changed how people looked at Big Tech. 
&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.080
“Especially in the United&nbsp;
States, I think, for a long time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.720
it was seen as a source of strength.
But the idea that there could be groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:36.880
inside the United States that were using those&nbsp;
alleged champions of the United States to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.560
their pride into a vulnerability, has&nbsp;
really hit home for a lot of Americans.”

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.560
Since then, stricter data protection laws&nbsp;
have been passed around the world.  
 &nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.160
The full truth is that they&nbsp;
haven’t always been enforced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.720
But increasingly, regulators and courts&nbsp;
are doing just that — and that’s one reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.200
why Big Tech is feeling the heat. 
 
The second one is that more attention is paid to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.760
liability — the question of who’s&nbsp;
responsible for what’s being said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:08.960
or done on the platforms run by Big Tech.
“Supporters of US President Trump have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:11.680
stormed the Capitol building to support his&nbsp;
latest effort to overturn his election defeat”&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.800
It’s a question experts have been debating for&nbsp;
years. But it only moved to the front pages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:23.840
when in January 2021, following online calls&nbsp;
for violence, a mob stormed the US Capitol. 
&nbsp;

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:32.320
“What happened on January 6 has definitely&nbsp;
mainstreamed this conversation,”&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:38.080
“But we should note that even before&nbsp;
the capitol storming, we were already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:43.280
talking about liability of social media platforms,&nbsp;
when we talked about Myanmar, for instance.”  
 

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.760
Years earlier, nationalists in Myanmar had&nbsp;
already used Facebook to foment violence against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:53.680
the country’s Rohingya minority.  
 
“Facebook didn’t even notice that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.776
because they didn’t have anyone speaking&nbsp;
the local languages and they didn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.776 --> 00:06:01.120
anyone monitoring the situation in Myanmar.”
“They have grown even faster than they could think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:05.200
about what kind of responsibility comes with that&nbsp;
growth — and that has been one of the problems.” 
 

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:06.960
Facebook has acknowledged mistakes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:10.000
Myanmar, hired more local language&nbsp;
experts and pledged to do better.  
 &nbsp;

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.200
But increasingly, governments say that&nbsp;
such pledges alone aren’t enough. Some,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.200
including Germany, have therefore passed&nbsp;
laws that force the platforms to quickly take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:26.000
down incendiary posts or face big fines.
And speaking of money — there’s a third reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.920
why Big Tech is under fire: Taxes. 
 
For years, critics have said that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:34.720
companies aren’t paying their fair share of&nbsp;
taxes — both when it comes to how much they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.400
paying and where they’re paying them.
“The problem here is that governments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.360
both in the United States and Europe, have&nbsp;
given them a lot of space to go to the lowest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:51.520
point of taxes — to use the loopholes to deploy&nbsp;
their armies of lawyers and accountants all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:57.200
over the world to place profits in other&nbsp;
parts of the world where they were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:00.960
made and to really move the accounting around.”
NGOs estimate that in 2020 alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:07.520
a group of 20 countries in the Global South missed&nbsp;
out on almost 700 million Euro from Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:11.920
and over 765 million Euro from&nbsp;
Google’s parent company Alphabet.  

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:17.120
“Big Technology companies like&nbsp;
Facebook, Google and Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.560
will soon be paying more taxes in France…”  
 
Some countries have introduced national taxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.880
to make the companies pay at least some&nbsp;
taxes where people use their services.&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:33.200
But many believe that a global tax is needed.  
 
International talks were stalled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.360
by the Trump administration.   
 
The Biden administration has signalled willingness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:44.480
to come back to the table. Some hope for a deal&nbsp;
by this summer. But that’s far from certain.  
 &nbsp;

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:50.000
And taxes aside, some people also say the&nbsp;
companies have simply become too big.  
 &nbsp;

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:56.320
Which brings us to the final issue: competition.&nbsp;
And, once again, it helps to go back in time.&nbsp;

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:04.880
This man is John D. Rockefeller. Over a century&nbsp;
ago, he was essentially what Jeff Bezos or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.560
Mark Zuckerberg are today. 
 
Rockefeller had built up an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.800
empire that controlled over 90&nbsp;
percent of oil in the U.S. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.240
This made him the richest man of his time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:24.640
But people said his success&nbsp;
was built on unfair practices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:32.400
And in 1911, the US Supreme Court ruled to&nbsp;
split up his corporation into 34 companies.&nbsp;

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:40.080
Fast forward by 110 years and you will hear&nbsp;
people say similar things about Big Tech. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.520
In late 2020, US Democrats said in a&nbsp;
report that every one of the “Big Four”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:51.040
runs a monopoly like “the kinds of monopolies&nbsp;
we last saw in the era of oil barons.” 
 &nbsp;

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:57.120
Companies, they said, effectively have to&nbsp;
sell their products on Amazon’s marketplace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:03.120
because they have no “viable alternative.” 
 
Apple, they added, gets to decide what software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:08.400
people use on their phones — and charges&nbsp;
“supra-competitive prices” to developers. 
 &nbsp;

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.200
Facebook bought up companies&nbsp;
like Instagram or WhatsApp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.360
whenever they were about to become serious&nbsp;
competitors, according to the lawmakers.  
 &nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:22.240
And Google has favored its own services&nbsp;
in its search engine and advertising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:26.000
business, the report said.
The companies reject those claims:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.800
Amazon has dismissed them as “interventions&nbsp;
in the free market.” Apple says it does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:36.320
overcharge developers. Facebook argues that all&nbsp;
its acquisitions were approved by regulators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:41.840
And Google says that, unlike what the report&nbsp;
claims, it still does have strong competitors.  
 &nbsp;

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:48.080
But this hasn’t stopped U.S. and other&nbsp;
governments from launching antitrust cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:50.880
against the companies.  
 
And some are even asking:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:56.960
What if we go back to what was done&nbsp;
110 years ago, and break up Big Tech? 
 &nbsp;

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:02.320
Make no mistake: That would be incredibly&nbsp;
difficult and messy, and it’s completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:06.880
unclear if any kind of break-up will&nbsp;
happen — but the debate is gathering speed.&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.360
“I don’t know to what extent&nbsp;
it would solve all the problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:14.480
we have with big tech.” 
 
“In some ways, breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:24.480
up a company is a remedy of last resort.” 
 
“It’s a threat to our democracy if any single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:30.480
company has so much power. And we already see&nbsp;
how much lobbying power they have and how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:38.000
money they’re spending trying to influence&nbsp;
not just politics, but NGOs, research.”&nbsp;

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.480
Around the world, Big Tech is spending more&nbsp;
than ever on lobbying: In Brussels, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:48.800
the Big Four spent at least 15 million Euro in&nbsp;
2020, according to the EU’s lobbying register. 
 &nbsp;

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:54.480
They have their reasons: The bloc is working on&nbsp;
landmark legislation to both curb the market power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:59.840
of Big Tech and hold companies accountable&nbsp;
for what’s happening on their platforms.&nbsp;

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:04.880
And it was against that backdrop&nbsp;
that the leaders of Germany, Finland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:09.116
Denmark and Estonia sent their letter in March. 
 
“As Europeans, we aspire to continue asserting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.116 --> 00:11:12.000
our democratic values and rules in&nbsp;
the digital era,” they wrote.  
 &nbsp;

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.480
And they concluded by calling on Brussels to&nbsp;
“put the weight of the European Commission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:17.440
behind this joint endeavour.” 
 
It was their way of saying:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:27.840
Time to get tough on Big Tech.

